                %li
                  %a{:href => "#as_a_models_for_neutron_star"} As a models for neutron star
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_instantons_calorons_solitons_monopoles"} Relation to instantons, calorons, solitons, monopoles
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesInSolidStatePhysics"} In solid state physics
          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Skyrme"} Skyrme
              had studied with attention
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/On+Vortex+Atoms"} Kelvin's ideas on vortex atoms
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "On+Vortex+Atoms#RanadaTrueba01"}> Ranada-Trueba 01, p. 200
              )
          %p
            A
            %em Skyrmion
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} soliton
            in certain (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+physics"}> flavour
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field+theories"} gauge field theories
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#RhoZahed16"} Rho-Zahed 16
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Skyrme62"} Skyrme 62
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
            in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light+meson"} light meson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+perturbation+theory"} chiral perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} mass gap problem
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FirstEightSkyrmions.jpg", :width => "660"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Manton11"} Manton 11
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SkyrmionB20.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed form
              %a{:href => "#FLM12"} FLM 12
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Skyrmions-from-Gudnason-Halcrow-2022.jpg", :width => "660"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#GudnasonHalcrow22"} Gudnason &amp; Halcrow 2022
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple Lie group
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
            denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+bracket"} Lie bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            and with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -,-\rangle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \;\colon\; \mathbb{R}^3 \longrightarrow G
          %p
            and the Skyrme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi L
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo −
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⟨
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 16
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{L}
                  \\;=\;
                  \-\tfrac{1}{2}
                  \\left\langle
                  U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U \wedge \star U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U
                  \\right\rangle
                  +
                  \\tfrac{1}{16}
                  \\Big(
                  \\big[
                  U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U
                  \\wedge
                  U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U
                  \\big]
                  \\wedge \star
                  \\big[
                  U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U
                  \\wedge
                  U^{-1}\mathbf{d}U
                  \\big]
                  \\Big)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^{-1} \mathbf{d}U = U^\ast \theta
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pullback
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maurer-Cartan+form"} Maurer-Cartan form
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            denotes the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Manton11"} Manton 11 (2.2)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Cork18b"} Cork 18b (1)
          %p
            A
            %em classical Skyrmion
            is a solution to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            which
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishing at infinity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi r
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(r \to \infty) \to e \in G
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+point"} extremizes
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                implied by the above
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_chiral_perturbation_theory Relation to chiral perturbation theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Diakonov"}> Dmitri Diakonov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Petrov"} Victor Petrov
              %em Exotic baryon resonances in the Skyrme model
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.1212"}> arXiv:0812.1212
              ,
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9789814704410_0004"} doi:10.1142/9789814704410_0004
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Multifaceted+Skyrmion"} The Multifaceted Skyrmion
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9710"} doi:10.1142/9710
          %blockquote
            %p
              It is astounding that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Skyrme"} Skyrme
              had suggested
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} his model
              as early as in 1961 before it has been generally accepted that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pions"} pions
              are (pseudo)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goldstone+bosons"} Goldstone bosons
              associated with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous breaking
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+symmetry"} chiral symmetry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD)
              has been put forward as the microscopic theory of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong interactions
            %p
              The revival of the Skyrme idea in 1983 is
              %a{:href => "Skyrmion#Witten83"} due to Witten
              who explained the
              %em raison d’ˆetre
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrme model
              from the viewpoint of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+perturbation+theory"} chiral limit
              when the light
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
              masses
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_u
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_d
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_s
              tend to zero, such that the octet of the pseudoscalar mesons
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion"} π
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kaon"} K
              , η become nearly massless (pseudo)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goldstone+bosons"} Goldstone bosons
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective
              chiral Lagrangian (EχL) for pseudoscalar mesons, understood as an infinite expansion in the derivatives of the pseudoscalar (or chiral) fields, encodes, in principle, full information about QCD. The famous two-term Skyrme Lagrangian can be understood as a low-energy truncation of this infinite series. Witten has added an important four-derivative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-term"} Wess–Zumino term
              to the original Skyrme Lagrangian and pointed out that the overall coefficient in front of the EχL is proportional to the number of quark
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} colours
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p
              Soon after Witten’s work it has been realized that it is possible to bring the Skyrme model and the Skyrmion even closer to QCD and to the more customary language of constituent quarks. It has been first noticed
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#DiakonovEides83"} 6
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#DharWadia84"} 7a
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#DarShankarWadia85"} 7b
              %a{:href => "#DiakonovPetrov86"} 8
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              that a simple chiral invariant Lagrangian for massive (constituent) quarks
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              interacting with the octet chiral field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 8
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A = 1, ..., 8)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi Q
                      %mo ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mo ∂
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo −
                      %mi M
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %msup
                                %mi π
                                %mi A
                              %msup
                                %mi λ
                                %mi A
                              %msub
                                %mi γ
                                %mn 5
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi F
                                %mi π
                      %mo )
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L} = \overline{Q} \left( \partial\!\!\!\!/ - M e^{ \tfrac{i \pi^A \lambda^A \gamma_5}{F_\pi}  }  \right) Q
            %p induces, via a quark loop in the external pseudoscalar fields (see Fig. 3.1), the EχL whose lowest-derivative terms coincide with the Skyrme Lagrangian, including automatically the Wess–Zumino term, with the correct coefficient!
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DiakonovPetrovQuarkLoop.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p
              The condition that the winding number of the trial field is unity needs to be imposed to get a deeply bound state, that is to guarantee that the baryon number is unity.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "#DiakonovPetrovPobylitsa88"} 9
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              The Skyrmion is, thus, nothing but the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong mean chiral field binding quarks in a baryon
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DiakonovPetrovMeanField.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %ul
            %li#DiakonovEides83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Diakonov"}> Dmitri Diakonov
                , Michael I. Eides,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chiral Lagrangian from a functional integral over quarks
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://jetpletters.ac.ru/ps/1483/article_22635.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DiakonovEides83.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#DharWadia84
              %p
                A. Dhar, Spenta R. Wadia,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Nambu—Jona-Lasinio Model: An Effective Lagrangian for Quantum Chromodynamics at Intermediate Length Scales
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.52.959"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.52.959
            %li#DarShankarWadia85
              %p
                Avinash Dhar, R. Shankar, Spenta R. Wadia,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Nambu–Jona-Lasinio–type effective Lagrangian: Anomalies and nonlinear Lagrangian of low-energy, large-N QCD
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.31.3256"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.31.3256
            %li#DiakonovPetrov86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Diakonov"}> Dmitri Diakonov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Petrov"} Victor Petrov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A theory of light quarks in the instanton vacuum
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(86)90011-8"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(86)90011-8
            %li#DiakonovPetrovPobylitsa88
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Diakonov"}> Dmitri Diakonov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Petrov"} Victor Petrov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Chiral Theory of Nucleons
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/247700"} spire:247700
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(88)90443-9"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(88)90443-9
          %h3#AsAModelForBaryonsAndNuclei As a model for atomic nuclei
          %p
            Skyrmions are candidate models for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            and even some aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
            (
            %a{:href => "#Riska93"}> Riska 93
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BattyeMantonSutcliffe10"} Battye-Manton-Sutcliffe 10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Manton16"} Manton 16
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NayaSutcliffe18a"} Naya-Sutcliffe 18a
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#NayaSutcliffe18b}"} Naya-Sutcliffe 18b
          %p
            For instance various resonances of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/carbon"} carbon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleus"} nucleus
            are modeled well by a Skyrmion with baryon number 12 (
            %a{:href => "#LauManton14"}> Lau-Manton 14
            ):
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SkyrmionB12.jpg", :width => "300"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed form
              %a{:href => "#LauManton14"} Lau-Manton 14
          %p
            See also the animated computations in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GudnasonHalcrow18"} Gudnason &amp; Halcrow 2018
          %p#IncludingTowerOfMesons
            For Skyrmion models of nuclei to match well to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pion+field"} pion field
            but also the tower of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+mesons"} vector mesons
            need to be included in the construction.
          %p
            Including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rho+meson"} rho meson
            gives good results for light nuclei (
            %a{:href => "#NayaSutcliffe18a"}> Naya-Sutcliffe 18a
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#NayaSutcliffe18b"} Naya-Sutcliffe 18b
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/SkyrmionsWithRho.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed form
              %a{:href => "#NayaSutcliffe18a"} Naya-Sutcliffe 18a
          %p
            An analogous discussion for inclusion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-mesons"} omega-mesons
            is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GudnasonSpeight20"} Gudnason-Speight 20
          %h3#AsBoundaryFieldTheory As a holographic boundary theory
          %p
            The Skyrmions in 4d spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} above
            = succeed "-contributions" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+meson"} vector meson
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT"} holographic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
            reduction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D5+Yang-Mills+theory"} D=5 Yang-Mills theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#SakaiSugimoto04"}> Sakai-Sugimoto 04, Section 5.2
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SakaiSugimoto05"} Sakai-Sugimoto 05, Section 3.3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sugimoto16"} Sugimoto 16, Section 15.3.4
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Bartolini17"} Bartolini 17, Section 2
          %p
            This phenomenon is essentially the theorem of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AtiyahManton89"} Atiyah-Manton 89
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sutcliffe10"} Sutcliffe 10
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Sutcliffe15"} Sutcliffe 15
          %p
            In this way Skyrmions (and hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atomic+nuclei"} atomic nuclei
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#AsAModelForBaryonsAndNuclei"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto+model"} Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-D8-brane+intersection"} D4/D8
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+model"} intersecting D-brane model
            described by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+correspondence"} AdS-QCD correspondence
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"}> holographic QCD
            ”).
          %p
            This way, via the equivalence between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-D8-brane+intersections"} D4-D8-brane intersections
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D8-brane"} D8-brane
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrapped
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D4-branes"} D4-branes
            (
            %a{:href => "#Sugimoto16"}> Sugimoto 16, 15.4.1
            ):
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BaryonsAsD4Branes.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Sugimoto16"} Sugimoto 16
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#as_the_ultimate_bag_model As the ultimate bag model
          %blockquote
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmion"} Skyrmion
              is the ultimate
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark+bag+model"} bag model
              with zero size bag radius, lending further credence to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cheshire+cat+principle"} Cheshire cat principle
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "Cheshire+cat+principle#NielsenZahed09"} Nielsen-Zahed 09
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
