          %h2#history History
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#RhoZahed10"} Rho-Zahed 10, Preface
          %blockquote
            %p
              Two path-breaking developments took place consecutively in physics in the years 1983 and 1984: First in nuclear physics with the rediscovery of Skyrme’s seminal idea on the structure of baryons and then a
              %a{:href => "string+theory#ReferencesHistory"} “revolution” in string theory
              in the following year.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
              at that time the most unconventional idea of Skyrme that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermionic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
              could emerge as topological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CF%80-meson"} π-meson
              cloud was confirmed in the context of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
              (QCD) in the large number-of-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c
              ) limit. It also confirmed how the solitonic structure of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nucleons"} nucleons
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
              — with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong interactions
              %a{:href => "string+theory#ReferencesHistory"} first string theory revolution
              which then took most of the principal actors who played the dominant role in reviving the skyrmion picture away from that problem and swept them into the mainstream of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              reaching out to a much higher
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scale"} scale
              %em per se
              but fortunate for nuclear physics, for it was then mostly nuclear theorists who picked up what was left behind in the wake of the celebrated string revolution and proceeded to uncover fascinating novel aspects of nuclear structure which otherwise would have eluded physicists, notably concepts such as the ‘Cheshire Cat phenomenon’ in hadronic dynamics.
            %p
              What has taken place since 1983 is a beautiful story in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+physics"} condensed matter physics
              %span.newWikiWord
                astrophysics
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/astrophysics"} ?
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original articles:
          %ul
            %li#Skyrme62
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Skyrme"}> Tony Skyrme
                ,
                = succeed "," do
